# Deployment Safety

## Context

Every deployment freezes a reviewed configuration and exact base/target SHA. A database reservation, shared environment lock, atomic claim and execution token protect against concurrent workers.

## Decisions

- Trace preview, confirmation, approval, enqueue, execution, cancellation and reconciliation before changing a state or candidate field. Update digest validation and worker checks with any new candidate metadata; do not mutate saved candidates.
- Require a clean canonical root, exact registered origin and branch, successful fetch, fast-forward ancestry and unchanged reviewed base/target. Never substitute the latest origin SHA at execution time.
- Preserve the typed command allowlist, POSIX quoting, disabled Git hooks and overwrite protection for ignored files. Do not add raw shell input, migrations, reset/clean, force-pull or implicit Composer scripts/plugins.
- Recovery is a new reviewed candidate linked to a failed deployment whose code was updated. It requires fresh preflight with HEAD and fetched origin at the failed target, fresh grants/MFA, current configuration and fresh independent production approval.
- Recovery skips the Git merge and reruns all selected pipeline steps. Show that replay explicitly; do not silently resume uncertain steps or offer recovery for unknown state without reconciliation.
- Recheck requester and approver grants, digest, expiry and configuration before execution and each step. Requesters cannot approve their own production or protected candidate.
- Keep reservation plus job creation atomic and enforce execution ownership when changing worker state. Duplicated jobs must not quarantine another worker's execution.
- Preserve partial results on known failure. Connection loss, timeouts, unexpected faults and final HEAD mismatch require unknown-state quarantine. Release quarantine only after an administrator's attestation and repository inspection.
- Buffer bounded whole-command output and redact known secrets before persistence. A selected health check requires verified HTTPS and a 2xx result without redirects.

## Verification

- Exercise normal success, partial failure/recovery, production approval, permission/configuration revocation, cancellation, stale SHA, duplicate execution and unknown-state quarantine for changes to this flow.
- Use real local Git tests for Git semantics and separate-worker tests for ownership changes; keep their fixture roots guarded and isolated from live data.
